new file mode 100644
index 0000000..04fc2f3
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,88 @@
+## Deployment
+
+Fizzy ships with a [Kamal](https://kamal-deploy.org) configuration for running your own instance on a single server. Everything runs in one container on one box:
+
+- Puma serves the web requests and runs the [Solid Queue](https://github.com/rails/solid_queue) supervisor for background jobs, including the recurring tasks in `config/recurring.yml`.
+- SQLite holds the application data, cache, queue, and Action Cable messages. The databases and uploaded files live on a Docker volume, so they survive deploys.
+- [kamal-proxy](https://github.com/basecamp/kamal-proxy) terminates SSL with a certificate from Let's Encrypt and renews it.
+
+The configuration lives in `config/deploy.yml` and `.kamal/secrets`. Fork the repository, fill in your hostname, your mail server, and your secrets, and run `bin/kamal setup`.
+
+### What you need
+
+- A Linux server you can SSH into as root, with ports 22, 80, and 443 reachable from the internet. 2 GB of RAM is plenty to start. Kamal installs Docker on it if it isn't there yet.
+- A DNS name for Fizzy pointing at that server. Let's Encrypt needs it to issue the certificate.
+- An account at a container registry, such as Docker Hub or GitHub Container Registry, and an access token for it. Kamal builds the image on your machine and pushes it there for the server to pull.
+- An SMTP server to deliver email. Fizzy signs people in with codes sent by email, so nobody can log in until this works. Any transactional email provider will do.
+- Docker running on your machine to build the image, and the gems installed with `bundle install`.
+
+### Fill in `config/deploy.yml`
+
+Each value to change is marked "Fill in" in the file:
+
+- `image`: the name of the image to push, for example `your-user/fizzy`.
+- `servers.web`: the IP address or hostname of your server.
+- `proxy.host`: the DNS name Fizzy is served at, for example `fizzy.example.com`. It doubles as `APP_HOST`, the canonical host for links in emails, push notifications, and webhooks.
+- `registry`: your registry username, and `server` unless you use Docker Hub (for example `ghcr.io`).
+- `env.clear`: the SMTP server, port, and username, and the address Fizzy sends email from. The port defaults to STARTTLS on 587; set `SMTP_TLS: true` for SMTPS on 465, and `SMTP_AUTHENTICATION: login` if your provider needs it. Drop `SMTP_USERNAME` for a relay that doesn't authenticate.
+- `builder.arch`: `amd64` or `arm64`, matching the server's CPU. Building for a different architecture than your own machine works, but is slower.
+
+### Provide the secrets
+
+`.kamal/secrets` lists the secrets Kamal passes to the container. Each one is read from your shell environment when you run `bin/kamal`, so nothing secret is committed:
+
+```sh
+export KAMAL_REGISTRY_PASSWORD=...   # access token for the container registry
+export SECRET_KEY_BASE=$(bin/rails secret)   # keep it somewhere safe: changing it signs everyone out
+export SMTP_PASSWORD=...
+```
+
+To enable browser push notifications, generate a VAPID key pair and export it too:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails runner 'k = WebPush.generate_key; puts "VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=#{k.public_key}", "VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=#{k.private_key}"'
+```
+
+Rather than exporting them by hand, you can keep the values in a `.env` file, which is gitignored, and load it with `set -a; source .env; set +a`. Or edit `.kamal/secrets` to read them from a password manager with [`kamal secrets fetch`](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/commands/secrets/).
+
+### Deploy
+
+Commit your changes first: Kamal builds the image from a clean clone of the repository, so uncommitted changes are left out. Then:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal installs Docker on the server, builds and pushes the image, boots kamal-proxy, and starts Fizzy. On its first start the container creates the SQLite databases on the `fizzy_storage` volume, and kamal-proxy fetches the certificate as soon as the first request arrives. A minute later Fizzy is up at `https://your-host`.
+
+Open it and sign up. The first signup creates the account for this instance and closes further signups; everyone else joins through the account's join link.
+
+To ship a new version, for example after merging changes from upstream, run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal deploy
+```
+
+Kamal starts the new container next to the old one, runs pending migrations, and switches traffic over once the new one is healthy. The databases and uploads on the volume carry over untouched.
+
+### Running the instance
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal logs      # follow the logs
+bin/kamal console   # open a Rails console
+bin/kamal shell     # open a shell in the container
+bin/kamal dbc       # open the SQLite console
+bin/kamal details   # see what is running
+```
+
+Everything worth keeping lives in the `fizzy_storage` Docker volume on the server: the databases in its root, and uploaded files under `production/files`. Back it up. For a consistent copy of a database while Fizzy is running, use `sqlite3`'s `.backup` command rather than copying the file:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal app exec --reuse 'sqlite3 storage/production.sqlite3 ".backup storage/production.backup.sqlite3"'
+```
+
+Puma starts one worker per CPU, and Solid Queue one worker process per CPU. On a small box, or if memory is tight, turn them down with `WEB_CONCURRENCY` and `JOB_CONCURRENCY` in `config/deploy.yml`. To move background jobs to their own container later, set `SOLID_QUEUE_IN_PUMA: false` and add a `job` role with `cmd: bin/jobs`, as described in the [Kamal documentation](https://kamal-deploy.org/docs/configuration/roles/).
+
+If Cloudflare or another CDN fronts the server, set its SSL mode to full (strict), so it connects to kamal-proxy over HTTPS.
+
+To deploy as a user other than root, install Docker on the server yourself, add the user to the `docker` group, and set `ssh.user` in `config/deploy.yml`.
diff --git a/test/deploy_config_test.rb b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..8c6dadf
--- /dev/null
+++ b/test/deploy_config_test.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,46 @@
+require "test_helper"
+require "kamal"
+
+class DeployConfigTest < ActiveSupport::TestCase
+  setup do
+    @config = Kamal::Configuration.create_from(config_file: Rails.root.join("config/deploy.yml"), version: "test")
+    @web = @config.role(:web)
+  end
+
+  test "runs everything in one container on one box" do
+    assert_equal "fizzy", @config.service
+    assert_equal %w[ web ], @config.roles.map(&:name)
+    assert_equal 1, @config.all_hosts.size
+    assert_nil @web.cmd, "the image's default command runs Solid Queue inside Puma"
+  end
+
+  test "terminates SSL at the proxy for the configured host" do
+    assert @config.proxy.ssl?
+    assert_equal 1, @config.proxy.hosts.size
+  end
+
+  test "links use the host the proxy serves" do
+    assert_equal @config.proxy.hosts.first, env.clear["APP_HOST"]
+  end
+
+  test "keeps the SQLite databases and uploads on a volume" do
+    assert_includes @config.volume_args, "fizzy_storage:/rails/storage"
+  end
+
+  test "delivers mail through SMTP" do
+    assert_includes env.clear.keys, "SMTP_ADDRESS"
+    assert_includes env.secret_keys, "SMTP_PASSWORD"
+  end
+
+  test "defines every secret in .kamal/secrets" do
+    defined_secrets = @config.secrets.to_h.keys
+    required_secrets = env.secret_keys + @config.raw_config.registry["password"]
+
+    assert_empty required_secrets - defined_secrets
+  end
+
+  private
+    def env
+      @web.env(@config.all_hosts.first)
+    end
+end
